    Reuse PostgreSQL result accumulation buffers when flushing small packets
    
    The native SELECT 1 completion path shrank a 16 KiB result buffer with realloc,
    queued the shrunken allocation and immediately allocated a new 16 KiB buffer.
    The result object was reused, but its accumulation buffer was replaced for every
    small result. DWARF profiling attributed 4.47 percent of sampled worker
    userspace cycles to this flush path, mostly allocator work.
    
    For results smaller than half the accumulation capacity, allocate a packet sized
    to the occupied bytes and copy those bytes into the output queue. Retain the
    accumulation buffer for the next result. Keep the existing ownership handoff
    and replacement allocation for larger buffers, avoiding a new large-result copy.
    Preserve empty-buffer behavior and reset buffer_used on both nonempty paths.
    Update the header documentation to describe retained versus transferred buffers.
    
    Keep the default capacity at 16 KiB so reuse can be measured independently of
    the proposed 8 KiB capacity experiment. Do not alter classification, diagnostic
    hooks, query digests or worker scheduling.
    
    Validation: optimized PROXYSQL31 build completed. An isolated ASan/UBSan harness
    using the actual original and patched reserve/flush functions verified payload
    identity, queue ownership and reuse across all lengths through capacity plus
    random reserve/flush sequences at both 16 KiB and 8 KiB. Repository tests were
    not modified. End-to-end performance measurement follows this source checkpoint.

